{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbdK6oCrKmsvwF5keRTn", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7289, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7289}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a59532ac-343b-4e5a-8774-77c7ddccf7e8", "uuid": "80128062-f8e7-4cd0-8c5a-dcebd4d27186", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T13:51:49.646Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbdK6Ss47DAd3vm7xUsT"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbdK6oCrKmsvwF5keRTn", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Banker's rounding** (also called round-half-to-even, or `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`) changes only one case: a value that sits exactly halfway between two candidates. Instead of always rounding that value up, you round to whichever neighbor is **even**.\n\n| Value | Round-half-up | Banker's (half-even) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0.5 | 1 | **0** |\n| 1.5 | 2 | **2** |\n| 2.5 | 3 | **2** |\n| 3.5 | 4 | **4** |\n| 2.345 \u2192 2 dp | 2.35 | **2.34** |\n| 2.355 \u2192 2 dp | 2.36 | **2.36** |\n\nAll other values round normally: 2.346 still becomes 2.35.\n\n**Why accountants like it**\n\n1. **It removes upward bias.** With round-half-up, every exact .5 goes up. Over thousands of line items, invoices or interest accruals, those small errors all point the same way and add up to real money. Under half-even, about half the ties go up and half go down, so the errors mostly cancel and totals stay close to the true unrounded sum.\n2. **Totals reconcile better.** If you round each line and then sum, the result is closer to rounding the sum. That means fewer penny discrepancies between subtotals and grand totals, and fewer \"rounding difference\" adjusting entries.\n3. **It's an established standard.** It's the default in IEEE 754 floating point and in Python's `round()` and `decimal` module. Many financial and regulatory contexts use it, so it's a defensible, auditable choice.\n4. **It's fair to both sides.** Neither party, such as a bank or a customer, wins every tie systematically.\n\n**Caveats**\n\n- It only matters for **exact** ties. With binary floats, a value like `2.675` isn't stored exactly, so it may not be a true tie. For money, use a decimal type such as `decimal.Decimal` or `BigDecimal`, not floats.\n- It isn't universal. Some tax authorities and jurisdictions require round-half-up, so check what applies before choosing.\n\nA stored project memory says tax calculations in this repo use `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`. I haven't checked that against the code. If you'd like, I can confirm where it's applied and whether every money path rounds the same way."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7289, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7289}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a59532ac-343b-4e5a-8774-77c7ddccf7e8", "uuid": "a3f50613-2529-428b-8b5c-275e12b536b5", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T13:51:57.478Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbdK6Ss47DAd3vm7xUsT"}
